{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:dmr --start-line 1 --lines 20'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:dmr\",\n  \"url\": \"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/5346\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:656c9e5eec79c9843e907a555ede2bf0a66a0db3515916af3d655080057c5a75\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"author README only, not the blocked publisher paper\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"656c9e5eec79c9843e907a555ede2bf0a66a0db3515916af3d655080057c5a75\",\n  \"total_lines\": 9,\n  \"start_line\": 1,\n  \"next_line\": 10,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"1: # DMR\\n2: Deep Match to Rank Model for Personalized Click-Through Rate Prediction\\n3: \\n4: This code for DMR is implemented based on the Alimama Dataset (https://tianchi.aliyun.com/dataset/dataDetail?dataId=56), references the codes of DIEN (https://github.com/mouna99/dien).\\n5: \\n6: \\\"alimama_sampled.txt\\\" is a sampled dataset for testing the code.\\n7: \\n8: Notice that, the user behavior data in this dataset contains only category, brand and behavior type, but no item.\\n9: \"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read taac2026-organizer-overview --find UniTokenFormer'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"taac2026-organizer-overview\",\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/junwei-pan/junwei-pan.github.io/blob/main/paper/TAAC_2026.pdf\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:762a70a7fb221d0c2802036a80179e350a02628351440f745113014160e40646\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived organizer overview with PDF page markers\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"7cd3a2d9076aa269739024eff82e956ccaa2b9c86348395a0bccb6996df2dce4\",\n  \"total_lines\": 860,\n  \"start_line\": 443,\n  \"next_line\": 543,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"443: Together, these scaling-friendly architectural and system optimizations enabled the model to scale from 100M to 1B\\n444: parameters, achieving an AUC of 0.83792 and winning second place in the Academic Track.\\n445: Academic Track: Third Place.The third-place team proposedUniTokenFormer, a unified token-enhanced frame-\\n446: work for post-click conversion rate (pCVR) prediction built on HyFormer [18]. Instead of treating tokenization as a\\n447: fixed preprocessing step, the method systematically designs token construction, target-aware enhancement, and capacity\\n448: allocation. Heterogeneous user-side and item-side fields are first concatenated and projected before being split into\\n449: multiple non-sequential tokens, preserving cross-field interactions as token capacity increases. Candidate-item tokens\\n450: further condition multi-domain behavior sequences and generate DIN-style interest tokens [59]. User, item, and interest\\n451: tokens are fused before domain-specific queries decode the behavior sequences with LENS-enhanced target-conditioned\\n452: gates and positional biases [48], while domain auxiliary objectives strengthen per-sequence supervision. The method\\n453: further treats token allocation as an explicit scaling dimension. The study showed that, under comparable dense model\\n454: sizes, increasing item-side tokens was more effective than increasing user-side tokens, while an item-heavy joint\\n455: allocation enabled a single-block model to scale to 1.063 billion dense parameters. Combined with field-wise feature\\n456: preprocessing, bidirectional time-gap encoding, grouped sparse/dense optimization, exponential moving averages, BF16\\n457: training, and graph compilation, the approach achieved consistent gains over the official HyFormer baseline.\\n458: Industry Track: First Place.The winning team proposedQueryFormer, a unified post-click conversion rate\\n459: (pCVR) model for jointly modeling heterogeneous non-sequential fields and multi-domain behavioral sequences.\\n460: Building on recent progress in unified modeling [ 27], the model replaces projection-only query construction with\\n461: explicit token-to-query attention. Its stackable field–sequence block first refines user and item field tokens with self-\\n462: and cross-attention. Item tokens then retrieve evidence from four behavior domains, while behavior-initialized probes\\n463: cross-attend to the refined field context to generate queries for subsequent sequence interaction. To scale capacity under\\n464: strict inference constraints, QueryFormer organizes tokenization as an embedding matrix with H independent columns.\\n465: These views are evaluated in parallel, and their sequence queries are packed into shared-parameter cross-attention to\\n466: amortize key/value projections and GPU kernel launches. Within the team’s experimental grid, increasingH consistently\\n467: improved validation AUC: the H=4 configuration gained 0.00066 AUC over H=1, while H=8 required only 1.89×\\n468: the per-sample inference latency of H=1. Replacing the cross-attention query generator with an MLP caused the largest\\n469: 9\\n470: \\n471: PAGE 10\\n472: individual ablation drop, supporting query generation as the main architectural contribution. QueryFormer achieved\\n473: first place in the Industry Track with an official test AUC of 0.83254.\\n474: Industry Track: Second Place.The runner-up team proposedUniDot, whose central component is the FM Highway,\\n475: a dedicated path that carries explicit dot-product feature interactions around the residual stack and delivers them directly\\n476: to the classifier [25]. Rather than computing second-order interactions only once at the input, as in FM [36] and its deep\\n477: variants, the model emits a factorization-machine term at every layer from that layer’s current token representations,\\n478: so interactions are re-derived as the representations evolve in depth. This places the design alongside a line of recent\\n479: industrial recommenders that reintroduce an explicit dot-product matching step inside an otherwise attention-based\\n480: backbone [47], but routes those scores past the trunk entirely, so explicit low-order signals are not diluted by repeated\\n481: nonlinear mixing and the classifier retains direct access to interaction evidence at every level of abstraction. The\\n482: highway allows the rest of the network to form a single homogeneous stack: because explicit interactions are guaranteed\\n483: a route to the output, the trunk is free to specialize in implicit mixing. The trunk stacks a repeated macro-block in\\n484: which a token-mixing bus and a sequence-retrieval bus (item tokens cross-attending to the behavior histories) operate\\n485: in parallel over a shared token space. The design is motivated by the observation that the embedding inner product\\n486: of collaborative filtering and the query–key score of dot-product attention [ 44] share the same primitive, while the\\n487: two buses exchange state at every layer through an MLP-Mixer [43] fusion. The sequence side is embedded once per\\n488: forward pass and shared by all consumers, bounding inference latency [47]. Training uses a dual sparse/dense optimizer\\n489: (Adagrad [9] on the embedding tables and Muon [19] on the dense parameters), an auxiliary conversion-delay head,\\n490: and multi-path mutual learning [56] across the model’s parallel prediction paths.\\n491: Industry Track: Third Place.The third-place team proposedUniBridge, a stackabledecode-then-mixarchitecture\\n492: for jointly modeling behavioral sequences and multi-field features. UniBridge first applies lightweight local temporal\\n493: modeling to each behavior stream. Queries conditioned on the candidate item then attend to the behavior history and\\n494: compress it into a small set of intent tokens. This design reduces the cost of long-sequence modeling and avoids\\n495: applying global self-attention to the full behavior history [2]. The resulting behavior tokens are combined with user and\\n496: item field tokens in a shared residual stream. In addition, a parameter-free RankMixer rewire [62] is used to model their\\n497: interactions. Compared with fully unified Transformer architectures [57], UniBridge keeps the number of mixed tokens\\n498: small and fixed. This design supports early interaction between sequence and field features with low computational\\n499: overhead.\\n500: Winner of the Unified Block Innovation Award.The awarded team proposed MaskRec, a topology-masked unified\\n501: architecture for jointly modeling heterogeneous feature interactions and multi-domain user behavior sequences. Building\\n502: on recent efforts to unify feature interaction and sequence modeling in industrial recommender systems [18, 57, 61],\\n503: MaskRec transforms heterogeneous non-sequential features, contextual signals, multi-domain behavior sequences, and\\n504: auxiliary representations into a shared token space and models them with a unified Transformer-style backbone. Its core\\n505: component, TopoMask, extends the masking mechanism of Transformer attention [44] from temporal constraints to\\n506: topology-aware interaction constraints, explicitly regulating token-level query–key connections according to token roles\\n507: and behavior-domain relationships. Global memory tokens and domain-level memory tokens are further introduced as\\n508: learnable aggregation nodes for global and domain-specific information. To inject candidate-conditioned interaction\\n509: signals before unified modeling, MaskRec further introduces DualQ, which performs bidirectional cross-attention\\n510: between user-side and candidate-item-side tokens, followed by asymmetric attentive pooling and domain-conditioned\\n511: feature-wise linear modulation (FiLM) [ 32] to generate domain-aware query tokens. The resulting design enables\\n512: source-local modeling, domain-level aggregation, and controlled cross-source interaction within the same backbone and\\n513: consistently outperforms the HyFormer-style baseline on the competition dataset.\\n514: Winner of the Scaling Law Innovation Award.The awarded team developed CoSFormer, a single-model architecture\\n515: for post-click conversion prediction built on the competition’s HyFormer-style baseline [18]. Its central contribution is\\n516: information-bearing token co-scaling. In the RankMixer-style backbone [62], model width factorizes asdmodel =T dsub,\\n517: where T is the number of query and context tokens. Rather than widening an unchanged representation, CoSFormer\\n518: fixes dsub = 4, appends tokens derived from new predictive signals, and widens the shared backbone in lockstep. The\\n519: resulting 48-token stream unifies static context and four behavior sequences through independently projected pretrained\\n520: vectors, explicit user–item crosses, TIN-based target-time readouts [60], and multi-resolution history summaries. A\\n521: 10\\n522: \\n523: PAGE 11\\n524: MaskNet-inspired dual stream [49], token-type-private feed-forward networks, and candidate-aware attention further\\n525: process these heterogeneous channels.\\n526: To isolate the scaling gain, the team evaluated a nine-step semantic-token ladder against two counterfactuals:\\n527: widening the existing bank and appending learned empty tokens at a fixed per-token subspace. Width-only scaling\\n528: degraded performance from the first scaling step, while empty tokens produced short-lived gains before turning\\n529: downward. Semantic co-scaling instead improved AUC until later token families became redundant, providing an\\n530: empirical stopping rule. The results showed that useful scaling came from growing predictive information and model\\n531: capacity together, rather than from token count or parameter count alone. Interestingly, this finding independently\\n532: aligns with the scaling philosophy of RankUp [5], which also expands model capacity by constructing richer and more\\n533: informative input representations rather than simply widening hidden layers or embedding dimensions. Together, these\\n534: independent results suggest that effective scaling in recommendation may depend not only on making modelswider,\\n535: but also on making the representations being scaledmore informative.\\n536: 6 Conclusion\\n537: This paper presents the KDD Cup 2026 Tencent Advertising Algorithm Challenge, a large-scale benchmark for\\n538: jointly modeling heterogeneous multi-field features and multi-domain user behavior sequences under controlled data,\\n539: computational, and inference constraints. Through two competition tracks and two progressively scaled rounds, the\\n540: challenge provides a common setting for systematically evaluating unified recommendation architectures and studying\\n541: their effectiveness, stackability, scalability, and hardware efficiency. The competition results reveal several recurring\\n542: design principles. Successful solutions increasingly move beyond conventional late-fusion pipelines by introducing\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --start-line 248 --lines 42'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 248,\n  \"next_line\": 290,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"248: L=−1N∑(𝒙,y)∈𝒮(ylogp(𝒙)+(1−y)log(1−p(𝒙))),L=-\\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}}(y\\\\log p(\\\\bm{x})+(1-y)\\\\log(1-p(\\\\bm{x}))),\\n249: where 𝒮\\\\mathcal{S} is the training set of size NN, with 𝒙\\\\bm{x} as the input of the network and y∈{0,1}y\\\\in\\\\{0,1\\\\} as the label, p⁡(𝒙)p(\\\\bm{x}) is the output of the network after the softmax layer, representing the predicted probability of sample 𝒙\\\\bm{x} being clicked.\\n250: 4.3. The structure of Deep Interest Network\\n251: Among all those features of Table 1, user behavior features are critically important and play key roles in modeling user interests in the scenario of e-commerce applications.\\n252: Base model obtains a fixed-length representation vector of user interests by pooling all the embedding vectors over the user behavior feature group, as Eq.(1). This representation vector stays the same for a given user, in regardless of what candidate ads are.\\n253: In this way, the user representation vector with a limited dimension will be a bottleneck to express user’s diverse interests.\\n254: To make it capable enough, an easy method is to expand the dimension of embedding vector, which unfortunately will increase the size of learning parameters heavily.\\n255: It will lead to overfitting under limited training data and add the burden of computation and storage, which may not be tolerated for an industrial online system.\\n256: Is there an elegant way to represent user’s diverse interests in one vector under limited dimension?\\n257: The local activation characteristic of user interests gives us inspiration to design a novel model named deep interest network(DIN).\\n258: Imagine when the young mother mentioned above in section 3 visits the e-commerce site, she finds the displayed new handbag cute and clicks it.\\n259: Let’s dissect the driving force of click action.\\n260: The displayed ad hits the related interests of this young mother by soft-searching her historical behaviors and finding that she had browsed similar goods of tote bag and leather handbag recently.\\n261: In other words, behaviors related to displayed ad greatly contribute to the click action.\\n262: DIN simulates this process by paying attention to the representation of locally activated interests w.r.t. given ad.\\n263: Instead of expressing all user’s diverse interests with the same vector, DIN adaptively calculate the representation vector of user interests by taking into consideration the relevance of historical behaviors w.r.t. candidate ad.\\n264: This representation vector varies over different ads.\\n265: The right part of Fig.2 illustrates the architecture of DIN.\\n266: Compared with base model, DIN introduces a novel designed local activation unit and maintains the other structures the same. Specifically, activation units are applied on the user behavior features, which performs as a weighted sum pooling to adaptively calculate user representation 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} given a candidate ad AA, as shown in Eq.(3)\\n267: (3)\\n268: 𝒗U(A)=f(𝒗A,𝒆1,𝒆2,..,𝒆H)=∑j=1Ha⁡(𝒆j,𝒗A)​𝒆j=∑j=1H𝒘j​𝒆j,\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{split}\\\\bm{v}_{U}(A)=f(\\\\bm{v}_{A},\\\\bm{e}_{1},\\\\bm{e}_{2},..,\\\\bm{e}_{H})&=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{H}a(\\\\bm{e}_{j},\\\\bm{v}_{A})\\\\bm{e}_{j}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{H}\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\bm{e}_{j},\\\\end{split}\\n269: where {𝒆1,𝒆2,…,𝒆H}\\\\{\\\\bm{e}_{1},\\\\bm{e}_{2},...,\\\\bm{e}_{H}\\\\} is the list of embedding vectors of behaviors of user UU with length of HH, 𝒗A\\\\bm{v}_{A} is the embedding vector of ad AA. In this way, 𝒗U​(A)\\\\bm{v}_{U}(A) varies over different ads. a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is a feed-forward network with output as the activation weight, as illustrated in Fig.2. Apart from the two input embedding vectors, a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) adds the out product of them to feed into the subsequent network, which is an explicit knowledge to help relevance modeling.\\n270: Local activation unit of Eq.(3) shares similar ideas with attention methods which are developed in NMT task(Bahdanau\\n271: et al., 2015).\\n272: However different from traditional attention method, the constraint of ∑iwi=1\\\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1 is relaxed in Eq.(3), aiming to reserve the intensity of user interests.\\n273: That is, normalization with softmax on the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is abandoned.\\n274: Instead, value of ∑iwi\\\\sum_{i}w_{i} is treated as an approximation of the intensity of activated user interests to some degree.\\n275: For example, if one user’s historical behaviors contain 90% clothes and 10% electronics.\\n276: Given two candidate ads of T-shirt and phone, T-shirt activates most of the historical behaviors belonging to clothes and may get larger value of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} (higher intensity of interest) than phone.\\n277: Traditional attention methods lose the resolution on the numerical scale of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} by normalizing of the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot).\\n278: We have tried LSTM to model user historical behavior data in the sequential manner.\\n279: But it shows no improvement.\\n280: Different from text which is under the constraint of grammar in NLP task, the sequence of user historical behaviors may contain multiple concurrent interests.\\n281: Rapid jumping and sudden ending over these interests causes the sequence data of user behaviors to seem to be noisy.\\n282: A possible direction is to design special structures to model such data in a sequence way.\\n283: We leave it for future research.\\n284: 5. Training Techniques\\n285: In the advertising system in Alibaba, numbers of goods and users scale up to hundreds of millions.\\n286: Practically, training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse input features is of great challenge.\\n287: In this section, we introduce two important techniques which are proven to be helpful in practice.\\n288: 5.1. Mini-batch Aware Regularization\\n289: Overfitting is a critical challenge for training industrial networks.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
