{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 /workspace/tools.py read unidot-paper-v1 --find 'FM Highway'\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"unidot-paper-v1\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2608.16797v1\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2608.16797v1\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"7714c79aba968b84d631bb677ca87a16614add7785fff830fd99b93e33b39727\",\n  \"total_lines\": 1342,\n  \"start_line\": 111,\n  \"next_line\": 211,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"111: token-mixing bus and a sequence-retrieval bus (item tokens\\n112: cross-attending the histories) run in parallel and exchange state each\\n113: layer through an MLP-Mixer fusion, while an FM Highway carries explicit\\n114: per-layer dot-product interactions around the residual stack directly to\\n115: the classifier. The sequence side is embedded once per forward pass and shared by\\n116: all consumers, bounding inference latency. Trained with a dual sparse/dense\\n117: (Adagrad + Muon) optimizer, an auxiliary conversion-delay head, and multi-path\\n118: mutual learning, UniDot finished as the runner-up on the\\n119: Industrial track of the TAAC ×\\\\times KDD Cup 2026.\\n120: Keywords: recommender systems, feature interaction, sequence modeling,\\n121: CTR/CVR prediction, token mixing, factorization machines\\n122: ††footnotetext: KDD Cup 2026 Tencent UniRec Challenge Workshop, August 12, 2026, Jeju, Korea.\\n123: Competition website: https://algo.qq.com/.\\n124: All experiments, data collection, and processing activities were conducted on\\n125: the competition host’s infrastructure. No experiments, data collection or\\n126: processing activities were conducted on Meta infrastructure.\\n127: 1. Introduction\\n128: Large-scale recommendation systems rank enormous candidate sets in real time and\\n129: underpin modern content and advertising platforms. Their predictive models have\\n130: matured along two largely separate traditions.\\n131: (1) Feature-interaction models—Wide&Deep (Cheng et al., 2016),\\n132: DeepFM (Guo et al., 2017), xDeepFM (Lian et al., 2018),\\n133: DCN/DCN-v2 (Wang et al., 2017; Wang et al., 2021), FiBiNet (Huang et al., 2019),\\n134: AutoInt (Song et al., 2019), Wukong (Zhang et al., 2024),\\n135: DHEN (Zhang et al., 2022)—learn explicit and implicit crosses among a wide,\\n136: mostly static feature set (user profile ×\\\\times item attributes).\\n137: (2) Sequential user-interest models, e.g. DIN (Zhou et al., 2018),\\n138: DIEN (Zhou et al., 2019), DSIN (Feng et al., 2019), SIM (Pi et al., 2020),\\n139: ETA (Chen et al., 2021), TWIN (Chang et al., 2023), LONGER (Chai et al., 2025),\\n140: and TIN (Zhou et al., 2024), model the dynamics of user behavior, typically\\n141: with target-aware attention over a single behavior history.\\n142: The TAAC ×\\\\times KDD Cup 2026 (Tencent Uni-Rec Challenge)—“Towards\\n143: Unifying Sequence Modeling and Feature Interaction for Large-scale\\n144: Recommendation”—targets exactly this gap, asking for a unified\\n145: tokenization scheme and a homogeneous, stackable backbone that models\\n146: sequential and non-sequential features in one architecture for large-scale\\n147: recommendation, ranked by AUC under an inference-latency budget (§A\\n148: describes the data). Following the recently introduced SlimPer\\n149: framework (Wang et al., 2026), we designed UniDot. It is a direct answer to this\\n150: brief: one stackable block, one shared token space, unifying both families. The\\n151: name abbreviates\\n152: Unified modeling via Dot-products Of\\n153: Tokens—cross-attention (sequence retrieval) and factorization machines\\n154: (feature interaction) are both dot-products of tokens, the single primitive the\\n155: whole model is built on.\\n156: This vantage point is deliberate. The FM inner product\\n157: ⟨vu,vi⟩\\\\langle v_{u},v_{i}\\\\rangle is the engine of collaborative filtering: user\\n158: and item meet through latent factors estimated from all observed\\n159: co-occurrences, so the score generalizes to user–item pairs never seen together\\n160: in training (Rendle, 2010)—and in advertising conversion data, with sparse\\n161: positives over a large, fast-moving ad inventory, most candidate pairs at serving\\n162: time are effectively new. Prior unification work makes this inner product\\n163: implicit—an emergent property of a deep interaction stack or\\n164: transformer. UniDot keeps it explicit wherever signal crosses the user–item or\\n165: candidate–history boundary, using the deeper machinery (token mixing, gated\\n166: MLPs, attention) to refine the operands of those dot-products rather than\\n167: replace the operation.\\n168: user / itemprofile fids+ pre-trained embsbehavioraldomains S1..S4S_{1}..S_{4}tokenizers (§4.2)𝐔,𝐈,𝐈h\\\\mathbf{U},\\\\mathbf{I},\\\\mathbf{I}_{h}seq pipeline (§4.3)embed once, share𝐇(1)..𝐇(S)\\\\mathbf{H}^{(1)}..\\\\mathbf{H}^{(S)}token-mixing busWukong / TokenMixer ×W\\\\times WMultiChannelSeqPoolMLP-Mixer fusion(FuseFFN)sequence-retrieval bus𝐈h\\\\mathbf{I}_{h} cross-attends each 𝐇(s)\\\\mathbf{H}^{(s)} + fusion FFN\\n169: macro-block ×L\\\\times Lclassifierρ\\\\rho + MLP →y^\\\\to\\\\hat{y}+ aux delay head[𝐔;𝐈][\\\\mathbf{U};\\\\mathbf{I}]𝐈h\\\\mathbf{I}_{h}viewspooledZmixLZ^{L}_{\\\\text{mix}}ZseqLZ^{L}_{\\\\text{seq}}FM Highway: [ϕ1;…;ϕL][\\\\phi^{1};\\\\dots;\\\\phi^{L}] (dots + Gram + cross-dots), bypasses fusionemb-skip signal eskipe_{\\\\text{skip}}\\n170: Figure 1. UniDot architecture (§4): two buses co-evolve through\\n171: LL macro-blocks, exchange state via the per-layer MLP-Mixer fusion, and the FM\\n172: Highway routes explicit per-layer dot-products past the fuser to the classifier.\\n173: UniDot stacks one homogeneous block LL times: two buses—a token-mixing\\n174: bus over user/item profile tokens and a sequence-retrieval bus whose\\n175: item tokens cross-attend the behavioral histories—exchange state each layer\\n176: through an MLP-Mixer fuser, while an FM Highway routes explicit\\n177: dot-product interactions straight to the classifier. Everything else\\n178: (§4) is how these pieces are made cheap, candidate-aware,\\n179: and scalable. Around this design—plus the training recipe behind our final\\n180: entry—we contribute four ideas.\\n181: (1) A dual-path, per-layer-parallel block: rather than cascading a\\n182: sequence module into an interaction backbone, each block runs two paths\\n183: concurrently—a non-sequential token-mixing path (a swappable\\n184: mixer slot: Wukong’s parallel LCB + FMB by default, a TokenMixer-style block as a\\n185: drop-in) and a sequence-retrieval path (item tokens cross-attend the\\n186: behavioral sequences)—which exchange state every layer via a canonical\\n187: MLP-Mixer fusion (§4.4), so profile and sequence signal co-evolve;\\n188: this interleaving is inspired by InterFormer (Zeng et al., 2025) and\\n189: Kunlun (Hou et al., 2026), from which UniDot differs in keeping the cross-boundary\\n190: signal an explicit dot-product.\\n191: (2) A shared, candidate-aware, multi-domain sequence pipeline: all\\n192: behavioral domains are embedded once and reshaped to a uniform per-position\\n193: width by a position-local fid-axis compression (§4.2); a DIN-style\\n194: conditionally gated SwiGLU injects candidate context into only the\\n195: gate, and a timestamp-interleaved merged stream models cross-domain\\n196: temporal patterns (§4.3).\\n197: (3) The FM Highway for explicit feature interaction: per-domain\\n198: query–key dot products, an aggregated Gram matrix, and cross-bus user–item dots\\n199: are concatenated across layers and fed directly to the classifier\\n200: (§4.4), bypassing the residual fusion path and preserving the\\n201: FM-style second-order signal a deep residual stack tends to wash out, at\\n202: negligible cost.\\n203: (4) Multi-path mutual learning over shared embeddings: we train\\n204: two UniDot paths jointly on one shared (and dominant) embedding table,\\n205: each regularized toward the other’s predictions (§4.6), pulling even a\\n206: single served path to a better minimum—a 1×\\\\times-cost model that would\\n207: itself place as runner-up (Table 1)—while the two-path mean\\n208: recovers the small remainder.\\n209: UniDot\\n210: finished as the runner-up on the Industrial track of the TAAC ×\\\\times KDD Cup\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
