n725: −- cross-bus dots only\\n726: 0.83570\\n727: 0.2156\\n728: −0.087%-0.087\\\\%\\n729: −- multi-channel seq pool\\n730: 0.83624\\n731: 0.2155\\n732: −0.033%-0.033\\\\%\\n733: −- token-mixing bus (identity)\\n734: 0.83590\\n735: 0.2161\\n736: −0.067%-0.067\\\\%\\n737: −- FuseFFN (no bus fusion)\\n738: 0.83679\\n739: 0.2154\\n740: +0.022%+0.022\\\\%\\n741: −- sequence cross-attention\\n742: 0.83604\\n743: 0.2153\\n744: −0.053%-0.053\\\\%\\n745: −- merged cross-domain stream\\n746: 0.83619\\n747: 0.2152\\n748: −0.038%-0.038\\\\%\\n749: 5.4. Scaling study\\n750: On this task the productive scaling axis is dense capacity, not sparse\\n751: (Table 5).\\n752: Doubling embedding width alone gave no A/B win—the high-cardinality tables are\\n753: not the bottleneck. Widening the dense path from 6464 to 9696 to 128128 improves\\n754: AUC monotonically with diminishing returns (+0.050%+0.050\\\\% cumulative). A second\\n755: identical path under mutual distillation (§4.6) is a more effective\\n756: use of dense capacity: +0.135%+0.135\\\\% at d=64d{=}64, more than the entire width sweep,\\n757: doubling dense parameters and FLOPs but not the embedding tables. The two axes\\n758: compose: d=128d{=}128 with two paths reaches 0.831960.83196 (+0.203%+0.203\\\\% over the\\n759: d=64d{=}64 single-path baseline), our submission; the all-data retrain then gives\\n760: 0.832170.83217.\\n761: Table 5. Scaling study.\\n762: Configuration\\n763: Dense par.\\n764: GFLOP\\n765: Test AUC ↑\\\\uparrow\\n766: Δ\\\\Delta\\n767: d=64d{=}64, sparse fold×\\\\times2\\n768: 18.8M\\n769: 5.9\\n770: —\\n771: no win\\n772: d=64d{=}64 (baseline)\\n773: 18.8M\\n774: 5.9\\n775: 0.82993\\n776: —\\n777: d=96d{=}96\\n778: 36.7M\\n779: 12.4\\n780: 0.83024\\n781: +0.031%+0.031\\\\%\\n782: d=128d{=}128\\n783: 60.3M\\n784: 21.2\\n785: 0.83043\\n786: +0.050%+0.050\\\\%\\n787: d=64d{=}64, 2-path\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
